@.str.65 = private unnamed_addr constant [40 x i8] c"show the contents of a store derivation\00", align 1
@.str.66 = private unnamed_addr constant [1554 x i8] c"\0A\0A# Examples\0A\0A* Show the [store derivation] that results from evaluating the Hello\0A  package:\0A\0A  ```console\0A  # nix derivation show nixpkgs#hello\0A  {\0A    \22/nix/store/s6rn4jz1sin56rf4qj5b5v8jxjm32hlk-hello-2.10.drv\22: {\0A      \E2\80\A6\0A    }\0A  }\0A  ```\0A\0A* Show the full derivation graph (if available) that produced your\0A  NixOS system:\0A\0A  ```console\0A  # nix derivation show -r /run/current-system\0A  ```\0A\0A* Print all files fetched using `fetchurl` by Firefox's dependency\0A  graph:\0A\0A  ```console\0A  # nix derivation show -r nixpkgs#firefox \\\0A    | jq -r '.[] | select(.outputs.out.hash and .env.urls) | .env.urls' \\\0A    | uniq | sort\0A  ```\0A\0A  Note that `.outputs.out.hash` selects *fixed-output derivations*\0A  (derivations that produce output with a specified content hash),\0A  while `.env.urls` selects derivations with a `urls` attribute.\0A\0A# Description\0A\0AThis command prints on standard output a JSON representation of the\0A[store derivation]s to which [*installables*](./nix.md#installables) evaluate.\0A\0AStore derivations are used internally by Nix. They are store paths with\0Aextension `.drv` that represent the build-time dependency graph to which\0Aa Nix expression evaluates.\0A\0ABy default, this command only shows top-level derivations, but with\0A`--recursive`, it also shows their dependencies.\0A\0A[store derivation]: @docroot@/glossary.md#gloss-store-derivation\0A\0A`nix derivation show` outputs a JSON map of [store path]s to derivations in the following format:\0A\0A[store path]: @docroot@/glossary.md#gloss-store-path\0A\0A{{#include ../../protocols/json/derivation.md}}\0A\0A\00", align 1
